  "prompt_text": "The figure is a whole-body overview linking the vascular system to major cardiovascular and related diseases across different organs. A semi-transparent human silhouette shows arteries in red and veins in blue, with callouts pointing to the brain, heart, lungs, kidneys, aorta, and leg vessels. The main message is that vascular disease is systemic rather than confined to one site.\n\nIn the brain, the diagram highlights conditions such as ischaemic stroke, intracerebral and subarachnoid haemorrhage, and vascular cognitive impairment or dementia, suggesting both blocked and bleeding vessel problems. Around the heart and chest, it marks dysrhythmias, cardiomyopathy and myocarditis, valve disease, hypertensive heart disease, congenital heart disease, and ischaemic heart disease. The lungs are linked to pulmonary embolism, emphasizing a clot traveling through the circulation. Lower in the body, the kidneys are associated with renal disease and the large central artery with aortic aneurysm. The limbs show peripheral artery disease and deep-vein thrombosis.\n\nOverall, the image summarizes how abnormalities of arteries, veins, and the heart can affect multiple organs, producing a broad spectrum of interconnected diseases throughout the body.",
